.TH RCPU 1
.SH NAME
rcpu, rimport \- run a command on, or mount a tree of, a 9front machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rcpu
[
.B -k
.I keypattern
]
[
.B -u
.I user
]
[
.B -h
.I host
]
[
.B -d
.I dir
]
[
.B -c
.I cmd
.RI [ arg ...]
]
.PP
.B rimport
[
.B -abc
]
[
.B -k
.I keypattern
]
[
.B -u
.I user
]
.I host
.I tree
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
These speak to a 9front cpu server as 9front's own
.I rcpu
and
.I rimport
do, through its
.B rcpu
service (port 17019): 9front's authentication,
.B p9any
with
.B dp9ik
(see
.IR factotum (4)),
then TLS with the session secret as a pre-shared key, then an
.I rc
script the server runs with the connection as its input and output.
They need a
.B dp9ik
key in
.IR factotum (4)
for the server's authentication domain, for instance
.IP
.EX
echo 'key proto=dp9ik dom=9front user=glenda !password=...' \e
	'auth=tcp!authhost!567' >/mnt/factotum/ctl
.EE
.PP
.I Host
is a name or address
(network
.B tcp
and port 17019 when not given).
.B -k
adds attributes to the key's query and
.B -u
names the user.
.PP
.I Rimport
has 9front's
.I exportfs
serve
.I tree
over the connection and mounts it on
.IR mountpoint ,
replacing what is there, or after
.RB ( -a )
or before
.RB ( -b )
it, with
.B -c
to allow creation.
.PP
.I Rcpu
runs
.I cmd
with its arguments, or an interactive
.B rc
when there is none, on the server, as the key's user, in directory
.I dir
if given (with
.B -d ,
else the user's home), with
.B $service
set to
.BR cpu .
As 9front's
.I rcpu
does, the server mounts what this end serves over the same connection at
.BR /mnt/term ,
and the command's input, output and error are
.BR /mnt/term/fd/10 ,
.B 11
and
.BR 12 ,
which are this command's;
.B /mnt/term/dev/cons
is bound over its
.BR /dev/cons .
9front's
.I rcpu
serves its whole name space there.
This serves a tree of its own with those files in it and nothing else of this machine:
.B fd/10 fd/11 fd/12
.BR dev/cons ,
.BR mnt/cpunote/data ,
from which no note is ever read (interrupts are not passed on), and
.BR env/rstatus ,
where the server leaves the command's exit status, which becomes
.IR rcpu 's.
.PP
Where this machine has a display, a 9front graphical program run this
way draws in a window here, as one run with 9front's
.I rcpu
from a rio window draws in that window; there is no flag for it.
9front's profile for
.B $service
.B cpu
binds
.B /mnt/term/dev
after its own
.BR /dev ,
and
.I rcpu
serves there, beside
.BR cons :
.TF dev/consctl
.TP
.B dev/draw
the draw device in a scope of the session's own (see
.IR draw (3)):
its programs reach the images they make and their window,
not the screen, another window or another program's images
.TP
.B dev/winname
the window's name.
The window is made when a program first reads this, so a command that
draws nothing has none; it goes when no program has
.B dev/mouse
open, when its close button is pressed (the session goes on), and with
the session
.TP
.B dev/mouse
the window's pointer, in 9front's form
.RB ( m ,
or
.B r
on a resize, then x, y, buttons and time in milliseconds);
a write moves the pointer
.TP
.B dev/cursor
the window's cursor, in 9front's form
.TP
.B dev/consctl
.B rawon
and
.BR rawoff ;
while a program has it raw, and no program reads
.BR dev/kbd ,
.B dev/cons
gives the window's keys rather than this command's input
.TP
.B dev/kbd
the window's keys as 9front's
.IR kbdfs (8)
gives them:
.BR k ,
.B c
and
.B K
messages, each a string ended by a zero byte; 9front's
.I rio
reads it, so
.B rcpu -h cirno -c rio
gives a 9front desktop in the window
.TP
.B dev/label
the window's label
.TP
.B dev/snarf
this machine's snarf buffer
.PD
.PP
It serves this machine's fonts too, read-only, at
.BR /mnt/term/fonts .
They are in Plan 9's format, rendered here from TrueType with 256 levels
of grey, and a 9front program uses one as it would its own:
.BR "acme -f /mnt/term/fonts/combined/go.16.font" ,
or
.B $font
in the 9front profile.
Nothing uses them unless asked; a program's first use of each
glyph sends eight times the bytes of a 9front bitmap's.
.PP
Both are refused in CNSA mode: 9front's authentication and its TLS are
classical cryptography (Ed448, ChaCha20-Poly1305, SHA-256), and this is
for working with 9front, not a part of CNSA 2.0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Mount a 9front file server's root and run a command there:
.IP
.EX
rimport cirno / /n/cirno
ls /n/cirno/usr/glenda
rcpu -h cirno -c 'cat /dev/sysname; ps | wc -l'
.EE
.PP
Run 9front's acme, or its rio, in a window here:
.IP
.EX
rcpu -h cirno -c acme
rcpu -h cirno -c rio
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/rcpu.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/rimport.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/rconnect.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cpu (1),
.IR dp9ik (2),
.IR factotum (4),
.IR import (4),
.IR draw (3)
.SH BUGS
Notes (an interrupt) are not sent to the far end, and 9front's
.B -p
(the
.I aan
filter, which keeps a session across a dropped connection) is not offered.
.PP
The window's keys are typed characters: a
.B k
message holds the character typed, not the key pressed, and a key's
release is sent at once, so a program that watches keys held down (a
game) sees each as a tap.
.B dev/wctl
and
.B env/wsys
are not served: a 9front program cannot move or resize its window,
or make another here.
